WEBVTT

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:07.539
Losing a bag should not mean losing the way to get it back. This is SeekerTag, running on a real Solana Seeker.

00:00:07.817 --> 00:00:14.051
I choose Continue with Seeker, select my wallet, and approve Sign In With Solana in Seed Vault.

00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:18.098
The app opens my items. My wallet is my identity.

00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:25.216
I add a new item, Seeker Backpack, choose its category, and add a private note.

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:31.088
The public message tells whoever finds it how to contact me, without exposing my phone number.

00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:36.889
I add a reward of zero point zero one SOL and set a one hour reservation.

00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:43.661
Before signing, the app shows the devnet network, wallet, transaction fee, and account creation cost.

00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:50.789
I approve the deposit in Seed Vault. After network confirmation, the reward is reserved on chain.

00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:58.249
Here is the unique QR code for this backpack.

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:05.899
I download the PDF and open its preview: six printable formats, with actual sizes and a scale check.

00:01:06.176 --> 00:01:13.003
The same item link can also be written to an NFC tag. This screen is ready for a physical tag.

00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:25.373
On a separate Android session, Alex opens that same tag as the finder. The reserved reward is visible.

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:31.601
No account is required to notify the owner. Alex enters a name and sends a message.

00:01:31.879 --> 00:01:39.132
Now both sides are on screen. I reply from the Seeker, and Alex receives it and answers from the second session.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:44.649
We arrange the return inside the app, keeping personal contact details private.

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:55.045
To receive the reward, Alex opens item details and confirms a receiving wallet.

00:01:55.323 --> 00:02:02.457
With Seed Vault, I sign a message proving ownership of the receiving address. It does not authorize a transfer.

00:02:02.736 --> 00:02:08.022
Back on the Seeker, I open the item from our conversation and choose Finish return.

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:15.861
The review shows the finder receiving zero point zero zero nine five SOL, and the five percent service fee.

00:02:16.139 --> 00:02:21.022
After confirming the simulated handover, I sign the payout with the owner wallet.

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:31.653
The transaction is confirmed on Solana devnet. The reward is released, and both conversations show the completed return.

00:02:31.931 --> 00:02:37.335
One tag, private coordination, and a verifiable reward. That is SeekerTag.

